{"entryId":"2902","paragraphs":[{"sourceId":"heilprin:sefaria-warsaw:ta:2902:1","sourceSha256":"67cedd7ccdee2f230a8e140668bb3319704f55cf46fba50a2c56b99e434bb9bf","englishSha256":"43842e06ce216ce357a6bbe638b2e9077a7a404c105bbb8a1578ada663363eef","citations":[],"inlineHebrew":[],"holds":[{"raw":"עבירם","reason":"The heading is Aviram, whereas the two attested patronymics say Amram. The exact status of the heading and the asserted shared father remain uncertain.","evidence":[{"reference":"Bekhorot 36b:22","target":{"url":"https://www.sefaria.org/Bekhorot_36b.22"},"witness":"William Davidson Edition - Vocalized Aramaic"},{"reference":"Chagigah 20a:5","target":{"url":"https://www.sefaria.org/Chagigah_20a.5"},"witness":"William Davidson Edition - Vocalized Aramaic"}]}],"notes":[{"text":"The Bekhorot passage expressly gives Ḥiyya bar Amram, and the corresponding headword has the same spelling.","references":[],"urls":[]},{"text":"Chagigah and Bava Batra explicitly name Yonatan ben Amram; the separate Heilprin headword cites both. This confirms the individual patronymic.","references":[],"urls":[]},{"text":"The heading is Aviram, whereas the two attested patronymics say Amram. The exact status of the heading and the asserted shared father remain uncertain.","references":[],"urls":[]}],"comparisonEvidence":[{"reference":"Bekhorot 36b:22","target":{"url":"https://www.sefaria.org/Bekhorot_36b.22"},"witness":"William Davidson Edition - Vocalized Aramaic"},{"reference":"Seder HaDorot 1279:1","target":{"entry":1279,"paragraph":1},"witness":"Pinned Warsaw digital witness"},{"reference":"Chagigah 20a:5","target":{"url":"https://www.sefaria.org/Chagigah_20a.5"},"witness":"William Davidson Edition - Vocalized Aramaic"},{"reference":"Bava Batra 8a:8","target":{"url":"https://www.sefaria.org/Bava_Batra_8a.8"},"witness":"William Davidson Edition - Vocalized Aramaic"},{"reference":"Seder HaDorot 1884:1","target":{"entry":1884,"paragraph":1},"witness":"Pinned Warsaw digital witness"}]}],"entryEvidence":[]}
